# Rendered by the Akamas FileConfigurator task into params.env, then sourced by
# apply_config.sh -> render_statefulset.sh, which validates every value and writes the vLLM
# flags (booleans become --x / --no-x: vLLM 0.31.0 parses them with BooleanOptionalAction
# and rejects --x=true). Every token must be in the study's parametersSelection
# (ignoreUnsubstitutedTokens false), and render_statefulset.sh refuses a leftover token or
# an empty value. Every flag below was checked against the v0.31.0 source
# (vllm/engine/arg_utils.py, vllm/config/{cache,scheduler}.py).
#
# GPU_MEMORY_UTILIZATION on the CPU backend = the KV budget (vllm/v1/worker/cpu_worker.py):
#   requested = ceil(total * f), total = min(NUMA node MemTotal, cgroup memory.max) = 48 GiB
#               (the pod limit; vllm/utils/cpu_resource_utils.py get_memory_node_info);
#   at init:    requested must be <= available = min(node free + file pages,
#               48 GiB - cgroup memory.current), else ValueError;
#   after the warm-up forward pass: kv = requested - RSS of the worker process, which must be
#               > 0 and <= available at that moment. On CPU the pod runs THREE vLLM processes:
#               platforms/cpu.py rewrites the 'uni' executor to 'mp'
#               (VLLM_ENABLE_V1_MULTIPROCESSING=1, the default), so there is an API server, an
#               EngineCore process and a separate MultiprocExecutor worker. cpu_worker.py
#               subtracts only the worker's own RSS, while the cgroup usage counts the worker
#               RSS, the EngineCore process RSS (torch import, estimate 0.5-1 GiB, unmeasured),
#               the API server RSS, the page-cached safetensors ~1.4 GiB and /dev/shm.
#   Qwen3-0.6B KV, bf16: 2 x 28 layers x 8 KV heads x 128 x 2 B = 114,688 B/token
#   (112 KiB, ~9.36k tokens/GiB); fp8 halves it (56 KiB). One 16,384-token sequence needs
#   1.75 GiB (bf16), the floor vLLM checks against max_model_len at startup.
#   Engine RSS after warm-up is NOT measured yet (estimate 3-5 GiB; the probe's E-gmu-low
#   run reads it from vLLM's "... GiB memory was consumed by non-kv usages" line):
#     f     requested  KV (RSS 3 / 5 GiB)   tokens bf16          tokens fp8
#     0.15   7.20 GiB   4.20 / 2.20 GiB      39.3k / 20.6k        78.6k / 41.2k
#     0.30  14.40 GiB  11.40 / 9.40 GiB     106.7k / 88.0k       213.5k / 176.0k
#     0.50  24.00 GiB  21.00 / 19.00 GiB    196.6k / 177.9k      393.2k / 355.8k
#     0.90  43.20 GiB  40.20 / 38.20 GiB    376.4k / 357.6k      752.7k / 715.3k
#     0.92  44.16 GiB  41.16 / 39.16 GiB    385.4k / 366.6k      770.7k / 733.3k  (vLLM default)
#   Demand of the load (lib_workload.sh): sessions of ~1.5k-token prefix + 3-6 turns of
#   ~384 in / 192 out, i.e. ~3-5k tokens of context per running sequence: 0.15 holds ~8-13
#   such sequences before preemption (pressure at any useful load), 0.90 holds ~75-125
#   running plus the idle sessions' cached prefixes (no pressure).
#   Upper edge: at 0.92 the pod holds ~44.2 GiB (worker) + ~0.5-1 GiB (EngineCore) + ~1.5
#   GiB (API server) + page cache + /dev/shm under 48 GiB: about 0-0.5 GiB at the
#   kv-allocation check, so the check can fail; 0.90 adds ~1 GiB (about 1-1.5 GiB at the
#   check). The probe's per-process RSS (node_facts.sh) and apply_config.sh's startup
#   summary (memory.current, per-process RSS) measure the real margin. Lower edge: fails outright if the engine RSS exceeds 48 x f
#   minus the 1.75 GiB a max_model_len sequence needs (f=0.15: RSS > 5.45 GiB).
#   Study domain (decided 2026-10-16): [0.15, 0.90], baseline 0.90, conditional on the probe
#   (E-gmu-low starts and reports its RSS; B-default at 0.90 starts with a measured margin at
#   the kv-allocation check). If E-gmu-high (0.92, the vLLM default) also starts with >= 2
#   GiB of working-set headroom, extending the top to 0.92 is a user decision.
# MAX_NUM_SEQS / MAX_NUM_BATCHED_TOKENS: CPU online-serving defaults 128 / 2048 (arg_utils.py
# get_batch_defaults, is_cpu). vllm/config/scheduler.py raises if max_num_batched_tokens <
# max_num_seqs (the study's parameterConstraint), and ALSO if chunked prefill is off and
# max_num_batched_tokens < max_model_len (16,384): with the domain [512, 8192] every
# ENABLE_CHUNKED_PREFILL=false configuration is rejected by vLLM, so chunked prefill is
# FIXED true (decided 2026-10-16) and not in the study; render_statefulset.sh still rejects
# the pair (exit 2, no cluster call) if someone sets it false by hand.
# BLOCK_SIZE: the CPU attention backend supports MultipleOf(32) only (cpu_attn.py), and AMX
# needs block_size % 32 == 0; CPU default 128 (platforms/cpu.py). {32, 64, 96, 128}.
# KV_CACHE_DTYPE: auto (= bf16) or fp8 (cpu_attn.py: x86 with AVX-512 or AMX, head_size % 32
# == 0; Qwen3 head_size 128).
# LONG_PREFILL_TOKEN_THRESHOLD: 0 = no cap (default); only acts with chunked prefill; must be
# <= max_model_len (16,384).
# STREAM_INTERVAL: FIXED 1 (vLLM default, decided 2026-10-16): a larger value batches streamed
# tokens, which the server-side TTFT/TPOT histograms (the goal's constraints) cannot see while
# the client does, so the optimizer could game the SLO with it.
# Not tuned on CPU (contract): async_scheduling (forced off by platforms/cpu.py), cudagraph
# sizes (cleared), enforce_eager / compilation mode (rewritten to DYNAMO_TRACE_ONCE),
# backends, TP/PP/DP, speculative decoding.
GPU_MEMORY_UTILIZATION=${vllm.gpu_memory_utilization}
MAX_NUM_SEQS=${vllm.max_num_seqs}
MAX_NUM_BATCHED_TOKENS=${vllm.max_num_batched_tokens}
BLOCK_SIZE=${vllm.block_size}
KV_CACHE_DTYPE=${vllm.kv_cache_dtype}
ENABLE_PREFIX_CACHING=${vllm.enable_prefix_caching}
ENABLE_CHUNKED_PREFILL=true
LONG_PREFILL_TOKEN_THRESHOLD=${vllm.long_prefill_token_threshold}
STREAM_INTERVAL=1
